        <span itemprop="definition">provides a schematic diagram providing a cross sectional view of an electrochemical cell comprising an anode (e.g., silicon, Li, Zn, ZnO, Graphite, LTO), a cathode (e.g., LiFePO4, LiCoO2, Sulfur, Ag, Carbon-Air, Carbon-Water), and having a separator wherein the super elasticity and/or shape memory effect of two high mechanical strength layers results in a very good mechanical contact between the separator and the electrodes.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0154">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">the high mechanical strength layer</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">is optionally a chemically resistant and thermally stable, electronically insulating layer with super-elasticity or shape memory effect (e.g., perforated shape memory polymer).</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0154">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">the low ionic resistance layer</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">may be very porous (e.g., greater than or equal to 80% porosity (e.g., PE)).</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0154">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">FIG. 10 J</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">provides a schematic diagram providing a cross sectional view of an electrochemical cell comprising an anode (e.g., silicon), a cathode (e.g., LiFePO4, LiCoO2), and having a separator wherein the conductive side of the separator reduces anode loss such as in silicon large deformations upon cycling which breaks the electronic contact between anode particles and the current collector and deteriorate the performance of the electrochemical cell.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0155">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">the high mechanical strength layer</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">is optionally a chemically resistant and thermally stable, electronically and ionically conductive layer (e.g., perforated metal), or an electronically insulating perforated layer (e.g., Kapton).</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="0155">
      </li>
